{"title":"1951 AHSME Problems/Problem 14","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem\">Problem</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1951_AHSME_Problems/Problem_14&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Problem\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>In connection with proof in geometry, indicate which one of the following statements is <i>incorrect</i>:\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/d/3/fd387c2c1e723940d817f07e46115be3a9f6531b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\textbf{(A)}\\ \\text{Some statements are accepted without being proved.}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"417\" height=\"17\" />\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/2/f/02fb6b052d019554e619e9912322155d90bbe539.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\textbf{(B)}\\ \\text{In some instances there is more than one correct order in proving certain propositions.}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"663\" height=\"17\" />\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/4/f/a4f56c4456a9d4f61709a971ef930c8530f91550.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\textbf{(C)}\\ \\text{Every term used in a proof must have been defined previously.}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"491\" height=\"18\" />\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/8/c/58cefcb26173ac11b0b734e355e5655270f17fe3.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\textbf{(D)}\\ \\text{It is not possible to arrive by correct reasoning at a true conclusion if, in the given, there is an untrue proposition.}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"867\" height=\"17\" />\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/e/8/6e878c4e0de4209f85f3d06cbd586ad7caef5712.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\textbf{(E)}\\ \\text{Indirect proof can be used whenever there are two or more contrary propositions.}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"624\" height=\"17\" />\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution\">Solution</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1951_AHSME_Problems/Problem_14&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: Solution\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/f/f/fffc7368e8e7b77c88b0b4bf732c88c60e27b439.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\boxed{\\textbf{(C)}}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -11px\" width=\"42\" height=\"30\" /> is false, as in geometry, the undefined terms point, line, and plane exist.\n</p><p>A is true, as in geometry, such statements (usually referred to as postulates or axioms) exist.\n</p><p>B is true, as in geometry, statements may be independent of each other, so it does not matter which order they are proved in. \n</p><p>D is true, as if the given or hypothesis of a theorem is false, then that theorem cannot be used.\n</p><p>E is true, as if two or more propositions are contrary, a proof by contradiction may be used to prove that not all of these propositions can be true.\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"See_Also\">See Also</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1951_AHSME_Problems/Problem_14&amp;action=edit&amp;section=3\" title=\"Edit section: See Also\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<table class=\"wikitable\" style=\"margin:0.5em auto; font-size:95%; border:1px solid black; width:80%;\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td style=\"background:#ccf;text-align:center;\" colspan=\"3\"><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1951_AHSME\" title=\"1951 AHSME\">1951 AHSC</a></b> (<b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1951_AHSME_Problems\" title=\"1951 AHSME Problems\">Problems</a></b> • <b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1951_AHSME_Answer_Key\" title=\"1951 AHSME Answer Key\">Answer Key</a></b> • <a rel=\"no